\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

This section describes the data, training protocols, and evaluation environments used to assess Kimodo-Policy. We first detail VLA policy pretraining on heterogeneous humanoid demonstrations (Sec.~\ref{sec:setup_pretraining}), and then describe post-training and evaluation on the HumanoidArena and SIMPLE simulators as well as on a physical humanoid robot (Sec.~\ref{sec:setup_posttraining}).

\subsection{Pretraining}
\label{sec:setup_pretraining}
\textbf{DataSet.} We assemble a 419-hour pretraining corpus from three publicly released humanoid datasets (Fig.~\ref{fig:pretraining_datasets}). HIW-500 provides teleoperated Unitree G1 demonstrations collected in real homes. From UniFoLM-WBT, we use the 14 task-specific G1 subsets available when our corpus was assembled: the BrainCo subsets \emph{Collect Plates into Dishwasher}, \emph{Make the Bed}, \emph{Pick Up Medicine}, and \emph{Pickup Pillow}; the Dex1 subset \emph{Put Clothes into Washing Machine}; and the Inspire subsets \emph{Clean the Living Room}, \emph{Collect Clothes (MainCamOnly)}, \emph{Pick Up Drinks}, \emph{Pickup Pillow (MainCamOnly)}, \emph{Put Clothes into Basket}, \emph{Put Clothes into Washing Machine}, \emph{Put Clothes into Washing Machine (MainCamOnly)}, \emph{Put Drinks into Fridge}, and \emph{Put Vegetables into Basket}. Main-camera-only variants are included when available, while UniFoLM-WBT entries released after this snapshot are excluded. Humanoid Everyday~\cite{humanoid_everyday} contributes 10.3K trajectories, over three million frames, and 260 tasks spanning dexterous manipulation, articulated-object interaction, human--robot interaction, and locomotion-integrated behaviors. We form training examples from an egocentric image, a language instruction, a 100-frame motion history, and a 50-frame future action chunk sampled at 30~Hz, converting trajectories to Kimodo's motion representation.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{image/pretrain.png}
  \caption{\textbf{Composition of the VLA Pretraining Corpus.} The corpus combines HIW-500, 14 task subsets from UniFoLM-WBT available when the corpus was assembled, and Humanoid Everyday. UniFoLM-WBT entries released after corpus construction are excluded.}
  \label{fig:pretraining_datasets}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Implementation Details.} We pretrain the policy for 1,000k optimization steps on 16 NVIDIA H200 GPUs, taking approximately 60 hours with bfloat16 mixed precision and a global batch size of 1024. During VLA pretraining, hand supervision is represented as a two-dimensional binary vector indicating the open/closed state of the left and right hands. This shared representation accommodates the different hand morphologies, degrees of freedom, and control conventions in the source datasets, preserving grasp-and-release semantics while providing a consistent target for the hand head. We use AdamW with learning rates of $3\times10^{-5}$ for the ControlNet backbone and $1\times10^{-4}$ for the ControlNet adapters and hand head, together with a weight decay of $0.01$. The learning rate follows a cosine schedule with 1\% warm-up and a minimum of 10\% of its initial value.

\subsection{Post-Training}
\label{sec:setup_posttraining}
\textbf{HumanoidArena Benchmark.} HumanoidArena~\cite{humanoidarena,sonic} provides seven leg-critical tasks: three human--object and four human--scene interaction tasks requiring coordinated locomotion, balance, posture adjustment, and manipulation. Its SONIC data contain 100 successful demonstrations per task (700 total) with synchronized egocentric RGB, proprioceptive states, and whole-body targets. We use all seven tasks for single-task fine-tuning and joint multi-task training, the latter being more challenging because one policy must acquire all tasks simultaneously. We evaluate closed-loop performance with SONIC using success rate (SR) and average fall rate (AFR), capturing both task completion and stability during whole-body task execution.

\textbf{SIMPLE Benchmark.} SIMPLE~\cite{simple} integrates MuJoCo contact dynamics with Isaac Sim rendering for scalable evaluation of humanoid loco-manipulation. The paper describes 18 tasks, while the original paper reports baseline results on six representative tasks: two using AMO and four using the SONIC tracker. We evaluate on eight tasks---Handover, Mobile P\&P, XMoveBendPick, CloseDoor, OpenOven, OpenFaucet, Push Office Chair, and OpenTrashCan. These tasks span object handover, mobile pick-and-place, articulated-object interaction, and household-object manipulation, providing a testbed for assessing policy robustness and execution stability in contact-rich whole-body control.

\textbf{Real-World Data Collection and Setup.} We collect real-world demonstrations with a teleoperation system implemented on top of and extended from the NVIDIA Isaac-GR00T data-preparation pipeline~\cite{grootn1}. As shown on the left of Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_setup}, the operator uses a PICO 4 Ultra headset and hand controllers together with PICO body trackers for whole-body and lower-limb tracking. The humanoid is equipped with an Intel RealSense D435i camera and Dex3-1 dexterous hands. We define eight real-world tasks spanning locomotion, balance, and single- and dual-arm manipulation, and record 25--30 demonstrations per task. The two plots on the right of Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_setup} show representative joint-position traces from two of these tasks: in Football Scoring (Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_setup}a), the right-leg trajectory changes sharply around the kick, while in Banana Cabinet (Fig.~\ref{fig:real_world_setup}b), the traces vary across the pick, place, and push phases. In both plots, solid curves show measured joint positions and dashed curves show the corresponding action commands for the left and right sides, illustrating the task-dependent motion captured in the demonstrations. We store the demonstrations in a LeRobot v2.1-compatible format, extended with Kimodo-specific motion and control fields for policy training with a motion prior.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{image/post-train.png}
  \caption{\textbf{Real-World Data Collection and Motion Profiles.} Left: PICO-based whole-body teleoperation of the humanoid. Right: measured (solid) and commanded (dashed) left- and right-side mean joint positions for two of the eight physical-robot tasks: (a) Football Scoring, showing a pronounced right-leg change during the kick, and (b) Banana Cabinet, showing motion across the pick, place, and push phases.}
  \label{fig:real_world_setup}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Implementation Details.} We fine-tune all policies on four GPUs using bfloat16 precision, 30\,Hz motion sequences, a 100-frame history, and a 50-frame prediction horizon. For HumanoidArena, single-task policies use a global batch size of 64 and are trained for 200k steps, while the joint seven-task policy uses a global batch size of 128 and is trained for 500k steps; the from-scratch and VLA-pretrained variants use the same fine-tuning configuration and differ only in initialization. SIMPLE policies use a global batch size of 64 and 200k steps, and real-world policies use a global batch size of 32 and 50k steps. Across these settings, the ControlNet has four layers and the root-control input to body prediction is detached. We use an MSE objective with weights of 4, 1, 1, and 0.2 for root motion, body motion, hand control, and hand transitions, respectively, and AdamW with a weight decay of 0.01. The ControlNet backbone, ControlNet adapters, and hand head learning rates are $3\times10^{-5}$, $1\times10^{-4}$, and $1\times10^{-4}$ for HumanoidArena and real-world fine-tuning; for SIMPLE, they are $1\times10^{-5}$, $3\times10^{-5}$, and $3\times10^{-5}$. All runs use a cosine schedule with a 1\% warm-up and a minimum learning rate equal to 10\% of the initial rate. For robustness, SIMPLE training applies color jitter, while real-world training applies color jitter and random image crops; SIMPLE uses continuous hand-control targets, whereas real-world training predicts binary hand commands conditioned on measured hand closure.
